#!/usr/bin/python3
"""The appliance's backups (tracker #33): encrypted for the office's recovery key, kept for a period, restorable.

    suveryn-backup run [--scheduled]         make a backup now (the hourly timer: only at the office's hour)
    suveryn-backup status [--json]           the last attempt, the last success, the backups on the target
    suveryn-backup list [--json]             the backups on the target, newest first
    suveryn-backup find --identity KEY       the backups on the target this recovery key opens, newest first
    suveryn-backup check FILE --identity KEY decrypt a backup and check every part, restoring nothing
    suveryn-backup key-test                  the administrator's test: the key left by the gateway opens the newest
    suveryn-backup request                   the administrator's request left by the gateway: back up now, the
                                             USB disks, a share's test, new settings
    suveryn-backup restore FILE --identity KEY [--yes]
                                             put a backup back on this appliance (a replacement machine)

**What a backup holds.** Both PostgreSQL databases, ``suveryn`` (documents and their passages,
conversations, cases, usage, settings) and ``keycloak`` (accounts, roles, sign-in settings), and the
office's own part of the data partition: ``/var/lib/suveryn/etc`` (``/etc/suveryn``: settings,
secrets, certificates), ``host``, ``network`` and ``nginx``. Not the software or the models: they come
with the image and the signed bundles. Not the appliance's identity: it is sealed to this machine's
TPM, and a replacement makes its own (the support contract moves with the office, #35).

**How.** Each part is written into one tar stream, compressed (zstd) and encrypted with age to the
office's recovery key, the ``recipient`` the web setup saved in ``/etc/suveryn/backups.json``. The
appliance holds only that public half: it can write backups, never read them. The office keeps the
private half offline; ``check`` and ``restore`` need it. Each appliance has its own key (the web setup
makes a new one each time). The file is written under a temporary name, hashed on the way, read back
from the disk itself (not its cache) and compared, then renamed:
``suveryn-backup_<name>_<UTC time>.tar.zst.age`` in ``suveryn-backups/<name>/`` on the target, with its
SHA-256 beside it (``.sha256``), which ``check`` and ``restore`` compare first. A failing disk is found
the night it fails, not on the day of a restore.

**Where.** A USB disk (its first partition, or the disk itself; ext4, exFAT, FAT or NTFS) or a share
on the office's network (SMB or NFS), mounted for the run only, ``nosuid,nodev,noexec``. USB disks may
be swapped (two disks, one kept elsewhere): when the chosen disk isn't connected, another disk with a
``suveryn-backups`` folder at its top is used, unless that folder is marked as another appliance's (its
``.office-recovery-key``, the public half, written on each disk used). A share's
password is in ``/etc/suveryn/backups-share.secret`` (0600) and goes to mount.cifs in a credentials file
in ``/run``, never on a command line.

**How long.** ``retention_days`` (14, 30, 90 or 365): those older than that are deleted before a backup
is written and again after, but the newest one is always kept. Then, if tonight's backup won't fit
(the last one's size and a quarter more), the run stops with "the backup disk is full" (step ``space``)
rather than half-writing it. Backups are the
outer bound of "deleted" in the data lifecycle (suveryn-docs ``administration/data-lifecycle.md``).

**Which backups a restore may use.** Those the recovery key given opens, under any appliance's name on
the target (``find``): a replacement has a name of its own until the restore gives it the old one, and
an office may have several appliances backing up to one place. ``newest`` is the newest of those; a
backup another appliance made is never offered, even when it is newer.

**The administrator's key test** (``key-test``): the office's key opens this appliance's newest backup
and every part of it is there; nothing is restored and the key is deleted after. The result goes to
``key-test.json``, which the gateway shows, with a reminder when the last good test is old.

**Restoring trusts nothing in the backup.** Anyone with the public half can encrypt a file for the
office, so a backup proves who may read it, not who made it. ``check`` and ``restore`` act only on
what this appliance expects: the archive may hold only the manifest, this appliance's own database
dumps and the four folders above; the manifest's databases must be this appliance's, its folders
among the four, its name a host name and its date a date. Owners and paths come from this appliance,
never from the manifest. Anything else and the backup is refused before anything is changed.
Nor is it trusted to be small: unpacking stops at 10,000 entries, 256 MiB of folders, or before the
data partition would be full.

**Test machines.** The folders are those on the data partition of the stick's layout
(``/var/lib/suveryn/etc`` and so on, which ``/etc/suveryn`` links to). A test machine installed with the
provision path keeps its settings in ``/etc/suveryn`` itself, so its backups hold the databases only and
nobody signs in after restoring one elsewhere; give it the layout first (VM check of #57).

**All or nothing.** The databases and folders are restored beside the live ones, the databases swapped
in one transaction, then the folders and the roles' passwords. The old databases and folders are kept
until every step has worked; a failure after the swap puts them back.

**PostgreSQL versions.** The manifest records the PostgreSQL major version the backup was made with
(16 on Ubuntu 24.04, 18 on 26.04; a backup without it was made with 16). A backup restores onto the
same or a newer version, so an office moves from a 24.04 appliance to a 26.04 one by a restore; a
backup from a newer version is refused before anything is changed (#57).

**A restore changes the live system at one moment, all or nothing.** The databases are restored
beside the live ones (``<db>_restoring``) and the folders copied beside theirs (``<folder>.restoring``);
only when every one has worked are the databases swapped in one transaction (the live ones renamed
aside, the restored ones into their place), then the folders renamed into place, then what they
replaced is dropped. A failure before the swap leaves the appliance as it was and says so.

**What the administrator sees.** ``/var/lib/suveryn/backup/status.json`` (0644, no secrets), which the
gateway shows: the last success, the last failure and its step, and the backups on the target.

Python 3 standard library; needs ``age``, ``zstd``, PostgreSQL's client tools, and for a share
``mount.cifs`` or ``mount.nfs``. Run as root.
"""

